const title = 'How Often Is an EICR Needed? | EICR Frequency | London BS Ltd';
const description = 'How often does an EICR need doing? Landlords every 5 years, homeowners every 10. Full guidance including the legal requirements for rental properties in England.';

const site = Astro.site?.toString().replace(/\/$/, '') ?? '';
const schema = [{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Article","headline":"How Often Is an EICR Needed?","url":`${site}/guides/how-often-is-an-eicr-needed`,"publisher":{"@type":"Organization","name":"London BS Ltd","url":site}},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":`${site}/`},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"EICR","item":`${site}/eicr`},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"How Often Is an EICR Needed","item":`${site}/guides/how-often-is-an-eicr-needed`}]}];
---
<Layout title={title} description={description} schema={schema}>
<section class="support-hero"><div class="container"><nav class="page-hero__breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>›</span><a href="/eicr">EICR</a><span>›</span><span aria-current="page">How Often Is an EICR Needed</span></nav><p class="page-hero__label">Guide</p><h1 class="page-hero__title">How often is an EICR needed?</h1><p class="page-hero__body">The answer depends on whether you are a landlord, a homeowner, or buying or selling a property. Here is a clear breakdown.</p></div></section>
<section class="section"><div class="container"><div class="support-content">
<h2>For landlords — every five years, or at each new tenancy</h2>
<p>Private landlords in England are legally required to have a valid EICR in place at all times. An EICR must be carried out at least every five years, and a new EICR must be obtained before a new tenancy begins if the existing one will expire during the tenancy.</p>
<p>For full details on the landlord requirement, see our <a href="/landlord-eicr" class="inline-link">landlord EICR page</a>.</p>
<h2>For homeowners — every ten years as a general guide</h2>
<p>There is no legal requirement for owner-occupiers to have an EICR at any particular interval. The generally recommended guideline is every ten years, though this is a recommendation rather than a rule. The age and history of the installation matters more than a fixed interval.</p>
<h2>When an EICR is advisable regardless of timing</h2>
<ul class="check">
<li><strong>Buying a property</strong> — particularly important for older properties</li>
<li><strong>Selling a property</strong> — can reassure buyers and avoid delays during conveyancing</li>
<li><strong>After flood or fire damage</strong> — always inspect before restoring the supply</li>
<li><strong>After major electrical work</strong> — confirms what was done and whether it was done correctly</li>
<li><strong>Moving into an older property</strong> — establishes a baseline if you have no information about the installation</li>
<li><strong>When problems appear</strong> — tripping circuits, burning smells, or flickering lights</li>
</ul>
<h2>Does a new consumer unit reset the EICR clock?</h2>
<p>Not exactly. A consumer unit replacement generates an Electrical Installation Certificate for the new board — it does not constitute a full EICR of the rest of the installation. Both documents cover different scopes.</p>
<h2>What if the previous EICR was unsatisfactory?</h2>
<p>If remedial work has been carried out following an unsatisfactory EICR, a new inspection should be done to confirm the faults have been resolved. The five-year clock for landlords runs from the date of the satisfactory certificate, not the original inspection.</p>
